# Recap

## The theoretical model

The previous model provided three key results:

- A static **incentive trap**: when rebates rise sharply with market size, monopoly rents $V_M$ can fall from a rise in $M$
- **Strategic substitutes** in R\&D effort across firms
- A **dynamic extension** in which period 1 outcomes lower the future cost of effort $k(\pi_{i,1})$

**This model was written for a single representative therapeutic class!**

## Results from RQ3 - FDA



```{r, RQ3}

# Load necessary libraries
library(tidyverse)
library(lubridate)
library(readr)
library(stringr)
library(ggplot2)
library(fixest)
library(knitr)
library(modelsummary)
library(kableExtra)
library(fixest)
library(TwoWayFEWeights)
library(DIDmultiplegtDYN)
library(did)
library(here)


# Load FDA approval data

fda_approvals <- read_csv(
    here(
        "processed_data/fda_approvals/fda_sdud_pdl_merged.csv"
        )
) |>
rename(
    approvals = n_approvals,
    firm = sponsor_name
    )

# Load patent data

patents <- read_csv(
    here(
        "processed_data/patents/patents_sdud_pdl_merged.csv"
    )
) |>
    rename(
        atc_level1 = ATC1,
        patents = num_patents)

# --- Build ex-ante demand --- #

# FDA

fda_ex_ante_stats <- fda_approvals |>
    filter(approval_year <= 2013 & approval_year >= 2010) |>
    distinct(atc_level1, approval_year, approval_quarter, prescriptions)

fda_ex_ante_total_prescriptions <- fda_ex_ante_stats |>
    summarise(
        total_prescriptions = sum(prescriptions, na.rm = TRUE),
        .groups = "drop"
    ) |>
    pull()

fda_ex_ante_demand <- fda_ex_ante_stats |>
    group_by(atc_level1) |>
    summarise(
        d_ex_ante = sum(prescriptions, na.rm = TRUE) / fda_ex_ante_total_prescriptions,
        .groups = "drop"
    )

# Patents

patents_ex_ante_stats <- patents |>
    filter(patent_year <= 2013 & patent_year >= 2010) |>
    distinct(atc_level1, patent_year, patent_quarter, prescriptions)

patents_ex_ante_total_prescriptions <- patents_ex_ante_stats |>
    summarise(
        total_prescriptions = sum(prescriptions, na.rm = TRUE),
        .groups = "drop"
    ) |>
    pull()

patents_ex_ante_demand <- patents_ex_ante_stats |>
    group_by(atc_level1) |>
    summarise(
        d_ex_ante = sum(prescriptions, na.rm = TRUE) / patents_ex_ante_total_prescriptions,
        .groups = "drop"
    )


# --- Build ex-post demand --- #

# FDA

fda_ex_post_stats <- fda_approvals |>
    filter(approval_year %in% 2014:2015) |>
    distinct(atc_level1, approval_year, approval_quarter, prescriptions)

fda_ex_post_total_prescriptions <- fda_ex_post_stats |>
    summarise(
        total_prescriptions = sum(prescriptions, na.rm = TRUE),
        .groups = "drop"
    ) |>
    pull()

fda_ex_post_demand <- fda_ex_post_stats |>
    group_by(atc_level1) |>
    summarise(
        d_ex_post = sum(prescriptions, na.rm = TRUE) / fda_ex_post_total_prescriptions,
        .groups = "drop"
    )

# Patents

patents_ex_post_stats <- patents |>
    filter(patent_year %in% 2014:2015) |>
    distinct(atc_level1, patent_year, patent_quarter, prescriptions)

patents_ex_post_total_prescriptions <- patents_ex_post_stats |>
    summarise(
        total_prescriptions = sum(prescriptions, na.rm = TRUE),
        .groups = "drop"
    ) |>
    pull()

patents_ex_post_demand <- patents_ex_post_stats |>
    group_by(atc_level1) |>
    summarise(
        d_ex_post = sum(prescriptions, na.rm = TRUE) / patents_ex_post_total_prescriptions,
        .groups = "drop"
    )


# --- Merge demand measures --- #

fda_approvals <- fda_approvals |>
  left_join(fda_ex_ante_demand, by = "atc_level1") |>
  left_join(fda_ex_post_demand, by = "atc_level1") |>
  filter(approval_year >= 2010)

patents <- patents |>
  left_join(patents_ex_ante_demand, by = "atc_level1") |>
  left_join(patents_ex_post_demand, by = "atc_level1") |>
  filter(patent_year >= 2010)


# --- Run regressions for FDA data --- #

fda_ex_ante_data <- fda_approvals |>
  select(-d_ex_post) |>
  rename(medicaid_market_share = d_ex_ante)

fda_ex_post_data <- fda_approvals |>
  select(-d_ex_ante) |>
  rename(medicaid_market_share = d_ex_post)

patents_ex_ante_data <- patents |>
  select(-d_ex_post) |>
  rename(medicaid_market_share = d_ex_ante)

patents_ex_post_data <- patents |>
  select(-d_ex_ante) |>
  rename(medicaid_market_share = d_ex_post)

```{r}

fda_ex_ante_data <- fda_ex_ante_data |>
  mutate(post = ifelse(approval_year >= 2014, 1, 0)) |>
  filter(atc_level1 != "P")

fda_ex_post_data <- fda_ex_post_data |>
  mutate(post = ifelse(approval_year >= 2014, 1, 0)) |>
  filter(atc_level1 != "P")

fda_rq3_feols_exante <- feols(
  approvals ~ medicaid_market_share : post : i(atc_level1) | 
  firm + atc_level1 + approval_year,
  data = fda_ex_ante_data,
  cluster = ~ firm
)

fda_rq3_feols_expost <- feols(
  approvals ~ medicaid_market_share : post : i(atc_level1) | 
  firm + atc_level1 + approval_year,
  data = fda_ex_post_data,
  cluster = ~ firm
)

fda_rq3_model_list <- list(
  "FEOLS: Ex Ante"  = fda_rq3_feols_exante,
  "FEOLS: Ex Post"  = fda_rq3_feols_expost
)

fda_rq3_models <- modelsummary(
             fda_rq3_model_list,
             stars = c(`*` = 0.05, `**` = 0.01, `***` = 0.001)
             )

fda_rq3_models

rm(fda_rq3_feols_exante, fda_rq3_feols_expost, fda_rq3_model_list, fda_rq3_models)

rm(fda_approvals)

```



## Results from RQ3 - Patents



```{r}

patents_ex_ante_year <- patents_ex_ante_year |>
  mutate(post = ifelse(patent_year >= 2014, 1, 0)) |>
  filter(atc_level1 != "P")

patents_ex_post_year <- patents_ex_post_year |>
  mutate(post = ifelse(patent_year >= 2014, 1, 0)) |>
  filter(atc_level1 != "P")

patent_rq3_feols_exante <- feols(
  patents ~ medicaid_market_share : post : i(atc_level1) | 
  firm + atc_level1 + patent_year,
  data = patents_ex_ante_year,
  cluster = ~ firm
)

patent_rq3_feols_expost <- feols(
  patents ~ medicaid_market_share : post : i(atc_level1) | 
  firm + atc_level1 + patent_year,
  data = patents_ex_post_year,
  cluster = ~ firm
)


patent_rq3_model_list <- list(
  "FEOLS: Ex Ante"  = patent_rq3_feols_exante,
  "FEOLS: Ex Post"  = patent_rq3_feols_expost
)

patent_rq3_models <- modelsummary(
             patent_rq3_model_list,
             stars = c(`*` = 0.05, `**` = 0.01, `***` = 0.001)
             )


patent_rq3_models

rm(patent_rq3_feols_exante, patent_rq3_feols_expost, patent_rq3_model_list, patent_rq3_models)

```




## The new puzzle from RQ3

- Most classes: significantly **positive** patent coefficients
- A and N (nervous system): **insignificant**, near zero
- Class P excluded

A single representative-class model cannot reconcile a **negative aggregate** RQ1 coefficient with **mostly positive** class-level coefficients.

# Adjusting the model

## Summary of changes

- Now index by therapetuic class, $c \in C$
- FOCs, auctions and the dynamic extension remains the same

| Before | After |
|---|---|
| Single representative class | Class index $c \in C$ |
| Generic $\tau(M)$ | Class-specific $\tau(M)$ with $\tau'_c >0$ |
| No link to competitive structure | $\tau'_c(M_c)$ increasing in substitutability $n_c$ |

## Class-indexing

Each class $c$ is now characterised by:

- A class-specific market size $M_c$ that shifts by $\Delta M_c \geq 0$
- A class-specific rebate function $\tau_c(M_c)$ with $\tau'_c(M_c) > 0$
- A measure of within-class substitutability $n_c \geq 1$

Assume that classes with more therapeutic substitutes face steeper rebate schedules.

## Why rebate steepness depends on $n_c$

The rebate slope can represent competitiveness:

- When the PDL committee has many interchangeable molecules to choose from, it can credibly threaten rejection unless deeper rebates are offered

Therefore, we should expect:

- Classes with more substitutes (high $n_c$) have steeper rebate schedules
- Classes with fewer substitutes (low $n_c$) have flatter rebate schedules

## Stage 3a — Monopoly

For class $c$:

$$
V_c^M = M_c(\bar p - \bar c - \tau_c(M_c))
$$

The monopsony channel varies across classes through the slope of $\tau_c$:

$$
\frac{dV_c^M}{dM_c}
= \underbrace{(\bar p - \bar c - \tau_c)}_{\text{Volume effect (+)}}
\; - \;
\underbrace{M_c \tau_c'(M_c)}_{\text{Monopsony effect (-)}}
$$

The sign depends on $\tau_c$ and $\tau_c'$

## Stage 3b — Duopoly

The auction equilibrium remains unchanged:

$$
b_c(c) = c + \tau_c(M_c)
+ \frac{\int_c^{\bar c}(1-F(u))\,du}{1-F(c)}
$$

$$
V_c^D = M_c \int_c^{\bar c}(1-F(u))\,du
$$


# The Class-Specific Threshold

## Defining $M_c^*$

Setting $dV_c^M/dM_c = 0$ defines the class-specific threshold $M_c^*$ **implicitly**:

$$
\bar p - \bar c - \tau_c(M_c^*) = M_c^* \, \tau_c'(M_c^*)
$$

At $M_c^*$, the marginal volume gain exactly equals the marginal rebate extraction.

A unique interior solution exists whenever:

- $\tau_c(0) < \bar p - \bar c$ (margins start positive), **and**
- $M_c \tau_c'(M_c) \to \infty$ as $M_c \to \infty$ (rebate extraction eventually dominates)

The trap binds in class $c$ iff:

$$
M_c^{post} \equiv M_c^{pre} + \Delta M_c > M_c^*
$$

## Result 1 — Substitutability lowers the threshold

Since $\tau_c'(M_c)$ is increasing in $n_c$, $M_c^*$ is **decreasing** in $n_c$.

- Classes with more therapeutic substitutes have steeper rebate schedules
- The monopsony channel turns negative at a smaller market size
- The trap is likelier to bind in competitive classes.

## Result 2 — Larger shocks cross the threshold more often

Classes with higher pre-expansion Medicaid share receive larger absolute shocks $\Delta M_c$, making it likelier that $M_c^{post} > M_c^*$.

Reductions in innovation should concentrate in classes that are **both**:

- Highly Medicaid-exposed (large $\Delta M_c$)
- Therapeutically competitive (high $n_c$, hence steep $\tau_c'$)

## Three regimes

The FOC ties effort to the **continuation value** $(1-Q_c)V_c^M + Q_c V_c^D$, which is dominated by $V_c^M$ when $V_c^M \gg V_c^D$:

| Regime | Condition | Prediction |
|---|---|---|
| Volume-dominated | $M_c^{post} < M_c^*$ | $de_c^*/d\Delta M_c > 0$ |
| Near-threshold | $M_c^{post} \approx M_c^*$ | $de_c^*/d\Delta M_c \approx 0$ |
| Trap-bound | $M_c^{post} > M_c^*$ | $de_c^*/d\Delta M_c < 0$ |

Strictly, $M_c^*$ is the threshold for the **monopoly channel** flipping; the effort threshold lies weakly above $M_c^*$, with the gap shrinking as $V_c^M / V_c^D$ grows.

## The rent gap as sufficient statistic

Effort is governed by:

$$
\Delta V_c \equiv V_c^M - V_c^D
$$

Two classes with identical $M_c^{post}$ can have **very different** innovation responses if they differ in $n_c$:

- High-$n_c$ class $\Rightarrow$ steep $\tau_c'$ $\Rightarrow$ compressed $\Delta V_c$ $\Rightarrow$ low effort
- Low-$n_c$ class $\Rightarrow$ flat $\tau_c'$ $\Rightarrow$ wide $\Delta V_c$ $\Rightarrow$ high effort

# Mapping to the DDD Results

## Regimes $\to$ predicted DDD sign

| Regime | Condition | Predicted $\beta_c$ |
|---|---|---|
| Volume-dominated | $M_c^{post} < M_c^*$ | $> 0$ |
| Near-threshold | $M_c^{post} \approx M_c^*$ | $\approx 0$ |
| Trap-bound | $M_c^{post} > M_c^*$ | $< 0$ |

## Empirical classification (RQ3)

- **Volume-dominated**: B, C, D, G, H, J, L, M, R, S → $\beta_c > 0$
- **Near-threshold**: A, N → $\beta_c \approx 0$

Class N is the diagnostic case: largest Medicaid volume, highest substitutability — exactly where the model expects the threshold.

## Why RQ1 and RQ3 disagree

RQ1 (aggregate) and RQ3 (class-level) are **not nested**:

- RQ1: single MedicaidShare $\times$ Post slope, pooled
- RQ3: class-specific slopes, ATC-saturated interaction

The pooled RQ1 coefficient is **not** a weighted average of $\beta_c$.

## Re-framing the puzzle

The aggregate RQ1 result is the **motivating puzzle**, not a target the model must match exactly:

- RQ1 negative $\Rightarrow$ contradicts the standard market-size prediction
- RQ3 + model $\Rightarrow$ the negative pooled estimate hides genuinely heterogeneous class-level responses

**Contribution:** aggregation conceals heterogeneity driven by class-specific procurement institutions.

## Mapping to the PDL counterfactual

| Outcome | Cash flow | Future $k$ | Predicted sign |
|---|---|---|---|
| PDL winner ($V_c^M$ or $V_c^D$) | $> 0$ | lower | **positive** |
| Partial success (clinical pass, auction lose) | $0$ | higher | **negative** |

New prediction (Result 3): the winner advantage is **largest in volume-dominated classes**, smallest in near-threshold ones.
